  <p>
    Note that in each diagram, we use four rectangles to estimate the area under <m>y = v(t)</m> on the interval <m>[0,2]</m>, but the method by which the four rectangles' respective heights are decided varies among the three individual graphs.
  </p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the value of
          <me>
            S = A_1 + A_2 + A_3 + A_4
          </me>
          by evaluating the function <m>y = v(t) = 0.25t^3-1.5t^2+3t+0.25</m> at appropriately chosen values and observing the width of each rectangle.
          Note, for example, that
          <me>
            A_3 = v(1) \cdot \frac{1}{2} = 2 \cdot \frac{1}{2} = 1.
          </me>
        For the left-most diagram, we see that each rectangle's height is determined by the value of <m>v(t)</m> at the <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>how heights are chosen for the left-most diagram</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice credit="1">left endpoint</choice>
          <choice>midpoint</choice>
          <choice>right endpoint</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer>
        of each subinterval of length <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>S = </m></label>$L4</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        For the middle diagram, we observe that each rectangle's height is determined by the value of <m>v(t)</m> at the <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>how heights are chosen for the middle diagram</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>left endpoint</choice>
          <choice>midpoint</choice>
          <choice credit="1">right endpoint</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer>
        of each subinterval of length <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>.  Using this pattern and the formula for <m>v(t)</m>, determine
          <me>
            T = B_1 + B_2 + B_3 + B_4
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>T = </m> </label>$R4</answer>
        </p>
      </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        For the right-most diagram, we observe that each rectangle's height is determined by the value of <m>v(t)</m> at the <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>how heights are chosen for the right-most diagram</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>left endpoint</choice>
          <choice credit="1">midpoint</choice>
          <choice>right endpoint</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer>
        of each subinterval of length <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>.  Using this pattern and the formula for <m>v(t)</m>, determine
          <me>
            U = C_1 + C_2 + C_3 + C_4
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>U = </m></label>$M4</answer>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li sectionWideCheckWork colorAnswersSeparately>
        <p>
          Complete the following sentences to explain why one of these estimates appears closer to the true distance the person traveled on <m>[0,2]</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since <m>v(t)</m> is always positive on <m>[0,2]</m>, we know that the exact area under the curve on the interval is the person's distance traveled.  Since all of the rectangles in <m>S = A_1 + A_2 + A_3 + A_4</m> lie <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>location of rectangles with respect to the curve in the first diagram</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>entirely above</choice>
          <choice credit="1">entirely below</choice>
          <choice>partly above and partly below</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer>
          the curve, it follows that <m>S</m> is an <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>how the area of the rectangles relates to are under the curve in the first diagram</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>over-estimate</choice>
          <choice credit="1">under-estimate</choice>
          <choice>estimate between the other two estimates</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer> of the true distance traveled.
          Since all of the rectangles in <m>T = B_1 + B_2 + B_3 + B_4</m> lie <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>location of rectangles with respect to the curve in the second diagram</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice credit="1">entirely above</choice>
          <choice>entirely below</choice>
          <choice>partly above and partly below</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer>
          the curve, it follows that <m>T</m> is an <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>how the area of the rectangles relates to are under the curve in the second diagram</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice credit="1">over-estimate</choice>
          <choice>under-estimate</choice>
          <choice>estimate between the other two estimates</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer> of the true distance traveled.  And finally, since all of the rectangles in <m>U = C_1 + C_2 + C_3 + C_4</m> lie <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>location of rectangles with respect to the curve in the third diagram</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>entirely above</choice>
          <choice>entirely below</choice>
          <choice credit="1">partly above and partly below</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer>
          the curve, it follows that <m>U</m> is an <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>how the area of the rectangles relates to are under the curve in the third diagram</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>over-estimate</choice>
          <choice>under-estimate</choice>
          <choice credit="1">estimate between the other two estimates</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer> of the true distance traveled, and thus <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>which of the three sets of rectangles best approximates the area under the curve</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>S</choice>
          <choice>T</choice>
          <choice credit="1">U</choice>
        </choiceInput></answer> provides the best estimate.
        </p>
      </li>
  </ol>
</p>
